[Event "Knight School"]
[Opening "Sicilian Defense: Alapin Variation"]
[Variation "Alapin Variation, Barmen Defense"]
[ECO "B22"]
[Character "positional"]
[Risk "low"]
[EarlyMate "none"]
[Tricks "early-queen"]
[Themes "tempo-gain,isolated-pawn,development-lead"]
[For "white"]
[Kind "sideline"]
[Opponent "6...Nf6"]
[Start "nc6-main-line 6.Nf3"]

6... Nf6 $6 { Natural, but it lets White hit the queen and then push the d-pawn into the knight on c6, winning more time. } ( 6... e6 7. Nc3 { Hits the queen. White's d4 pawn has no pawns beside it now, so White plays actively: bishop to c4, castle, rook to e1, and look for d4-d5 to open lines. } 7... Qd6 ( 7... Qd8 8. Bc4 ) ( 7... Qa5 8. Bc4 ) ( 7... Bb4 8. Bd3 { Develops and gets ready to castle. If Black takes on c3, bxc3 guards d4 again with a pawn. } ) 8. Bc4 ) ( 6... g6 7. Nc3 Qa5 8. d5 { The same idea as after 6...Nf6: the knight on c6 has to move again and Black falls further behind in development. } ) 7. Nc3 { Hits the queen. Now that the c-pawn has gone, c3 is the knight's best square and it comes with gain of time. } 7... Qa5 ( 7... Qd6 8. Nb5 { Hits the queen again and threatens Nc7+, which would fork the king and the rook on a8. } ) ( 7... Qd8 8. d5 Nb8 9. Bc4 ) ( 7... Qh5 8. d5 Ne5 9. Be2 { Blocks the line from the queen on h5 to White's queen, so the knight on f3 is no longer pinned and is guarded twice. } ) 8. d5 { The queen has left the d-file, so nothing stops this push. It attacks the knight on c6, and every square it can go to is worse. } 8... Nb8 9. Bc4 { Develops with an eye on f7 and guards the pawn on d5. White is three pieces out and ready to castle; Black has only a knight and the queen out. } 9... e6 10. O-O { Plan: keep the pawn on d5 for as long as it cramps Black, bring a rook to e1, and if Black takes on d5, take back with a piece and attack the king in the centre. } *
